        'ref(object) calls reactive() on the object internally, so ref works for everything — the common team convention is "ref everywhere" for consistency.',
        'Template auto-unwrapping: top-level refs in templates need no .value — the compiler/proxy handles it. But refs nested inside plain objects or arrays are NOT unwrapped in templates; refs inside reactive() objects ARE deep-unwrapped on access.',
        'Destructure pitfall #1: `const { count } = reactive({ count: 0 })` copies the primitive value — count is now a dead number, no Proxy involved. Same for passing state.count as a function argument.',
        'Destructure pitfall #2: spreading a reactive object (`{ ...state }`) snapshots values and disconnects them. Fix: toRefs(state) converts each property to a ref linked back to the source, so destructured refs stay live.',
        'Reassignment pitfall: you cannot replace a whole reactive object (`state = reactive(newObj)`) — consumers still hold the old Proxy. refs handle replacement naturally (`myRef.value = newObj`), one reason refs are the recommended default.',
        'Escape hatches: toRef(obj, "key") for a single linked ref, unref()/toValue() to normalize maybe-refs in composables, isRef/isReactive/toRaw for introspection.',
